WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.700
gRPC is a service communication model, not a security boundary by itself.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:16.200
It gives .NET teams a structured way to define remote procedures, generate clients, serialize messages with Protobuf, and communicate over HTTP/2.

3
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:27.180
Those features improve consistency and performance, but they do not decide who may call a service, which methods are protected, what data is valid, or how much work a call may consume.

4
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:42.500
Most .NET gRPC services run on ASP.NET Core, which means they can use familiar platform features such as TLS, authentication handlers, authorization policies, logging, dependency injection, and middleware.

5
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:52.600
The important design work is connecting those platform features to the service contract and method behavior rather than assuming that generated code or internal service location provides enough protection.

6
00:00:52.880 --> 00:01:01.280
Service-to-service traffic still needs explicit identity, authorization, validation, resource controls, logging, and deployment review.

7
00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:09.940
A call that originates from another service may still cross a trust boundary between workloads, teams, tenants, environments, networks, or privilege levels.

8
00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:16.920
Internal network location alone should not be treated as proof that the caller is allowed to perform every operation.

9
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:30.080
A secure gRPC baseline includes transport security, service identity, method authorization, contract review, message limits, deadline behavior, safe errors, and observability.

10
00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:38.820
These controls help teams understand who called the service, what method was used, what data was accepted, how much work was performed, and what happened when something failed.

11
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:46.300
The practical posture is to treat gRPC as a strong communication framework and then design the security model around it.

12
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:58.960
Reviewers should be able to trace the path from client configuration to channel security, identity, metadata, method policy, validation, resource limits, error behavior, and operational signals.
